content:
A Personal Sender Account holder who wants a higher, review-gated verification level asks Paloma compliance for a limit review. When the customer requests a limit review, the request is lodged with a decision window — a whole number of Paloma business days — and the decision due date that compliance commits to is fixed from that window.

## What a lodged request is

A lodged limit review request is recorded on the account history, dated the day it is lodged, naming the level requested and carrying its decision due date. It is recorded as lodged through the app, and it stands on the account history as a limit review request that compliance has not yet decided for as long as compliance has recorded no decision on it. Lodging a request changes no limit, level, document or transfer. The customer sees the request, its reference number and its decision due date in the app.

## How it is invoked

The limit review request is the customer's own act. The agent unlocks the tool `lodge_limit_review_54a88584` (with `unlock_user_discoverable_tool`) and hands it over, and the customer themselves runs it in their own app session.

## Parameters

| Parameter | What it is | Format |
|---|---|---|
| `sender_account_id` | the Personal Sender Account reference number of the account the review is for, as returned by the Personal Sender Account lookup for this customer | an account reference number |
| `requested_level` | the official name of the verification level the review asks for | exactly one value of the set below |
| `decision_window_business_days` | the decision window, as a whole number of Paloma business days | an integer of 1 or more, no fractional part; the tool applies no rounding |

Values of `requested_level`:

- `Paloma Enhanced` — the request asks compliance for the Enhanced level (level 4);
- `Paloma Premier` — the request asks for the Premier level (level 5);
- `Paloma Private Client` — the request asks for the Private Client level (level 6);
- `Paloma Starter` — names the entry level, which is held without any compliance review;
- `Paloma Verified` — names level 2, which is reached without a compliance review;
- `Paloma Verified Plus` — names level 3, which is reached without a compliance review;
- `none` — the marker the account history carries on entries that are not limit review requests, naming no level at all.

The decision due date recorded is the date, in YYYY-MM-DD form on Paloma's operations calendar (US Central Time), reached by counting `decision_window_business_days` Paloma business days (Monday to Friday, excluding US Federal Reserve holidays) forward from today's date, today not counted.

## Return

A successful call returns a text rendering of a dictionary with these fields:

- `account_event_id` — the reference number of the new limit review request;
- `requested_level` — the level named in the request;
- `request_date` — today's date, YYYY-MM-DD;
- `decision_due_date` — the date by which compliance must decide, YYYY-MM-DD;
- `status` — "lodged" when the request was recorded.

A receipt with status "lodged" and an `account_event_id` means the undecided request now stands on the account history.

## Illustration (hypothetical values)

A call with `requested_level` set to `Paloma Premier` and `decision_window_business_days` set to 4, run on a Monday in a week with no US Federal Reserve holiday, returns status "lodged", that Monday as `request_date` and the Friday of the same week as `decision_due_date`. The window value is an arbitrary input chosen for the illustration.
